# podium — the one slab the whole building stands on: plinth, rusticated die, cornice, terrace.
#
# Top at y = 1.80, the main floor. ONE slab, x -16.65..16.65 and z -13.05..8.10, so that the
# portico, both wings, the main block and the head of the great steps all land on the same piece
# of stone. An earlier facility had three slabs with the stair hanging over the gap between them;
# there is no gap here to hang over.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD — two things, and they pull against each other on purpose.
#
# 1. THE LARGEST SOLID MASS IN THE BUILDING. 33.30 x 21.15 x 2.25 m, 1555.25 cubic metres,
#    50,962,476 voxels at the contract's metre 32, in ONE component. Almost all of it is
#    interior: one material, nothing to look at. That is the point. It is the storage layer's
#    cheapest case — the uniform brick, eight bytes of payload against two kilobytes for a brick
#    where every voxel differs (see the budget note at src/app/main.cpp:2460) — and it is the only
#    thing in this scene that produces it in bulk. If uniform bricks ever stop being uniform, or
#    stop being cheap, this is the part whose memory figure will say so first, and loudest.
#
#    IT DOES NOT ACTUALLY GET UNIFORM BRICKS TODAY, AND THAT IS NOT THIS FILE'S DOING.
#    `apply_variation` in src/forge/sample.cpp walks EVERY non-air voxel, interior included, and
#    mints a per-voxel perturbed type unless `variation.by` evaluates to zero there. The manifest
#    ends with `variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070 seed=17` and no `by=`, so all fifty million
#    voxels in here are hashed apart and not one brick in the facility can take Form::Uniform.
#    The one-line fix is a `by=` that is zero away from the surface, and it belongs in
#    clips/facility.clip, not here; it is written up in clips/facility/requests/podium.md. What
#    this part can do is provide the large single-material volume for it to work on, and it does.
#
#    THEREFORE THIS PART IS NOT WEATHERED, and that is a decision, not an oversight. `weather`
#    displaces and recolours by geometry, and coating the largest mass in the building would turn
#    the one clean uniform-storage case in the scene into millions of per-voxel exceptions. The
#    walls, the steps and the site are outdoor stone too and are better placed to carry the
#    weathering share. If the podium must be weathered, scope it to `podium_zone_base` and expect
#    to explain what the scene tests instead.
#
#    It is also why the whole base is ONE material. See the paint section at the bottom.
#
# 2. DEEP NARROW GROOVES — about twelve hundred metres of them — cut into that mass.
#
#      rustication joints     0.045 m wide (M/10) and 0.090 m deep (M/5)
#      terrace paving joints  0.0375 m wide (M/12) and 0.045 m deep (M/10)
#
#    At metre 32 a voxel is 0.03125 m. So a rustication channel is ONE AND A HALF voxels across
#    and just under three deep, and a paving joint is ONE AND A FIFTH across and one and a half
#    deep. A groove is the hardest kind of thin matter there is: unlike a fin, it is a surface
#    that folds back on itself inside a single sample step, so a sampler that steps by its own
#    reported distance can walk clean over the mouth of one and never learn it was there. If the
#    contact sheet shows the rustication as a smudge or the paving as nothing at all, the SAMPLER
#    is what is wrong, not the grooves.
#
#    DO NOT WIDEN THEM. 0.045 and 0.0375 are M/10 and M/12, they are module fractions, and they
#    are at the limit on purpose. A podium with comfortable 0.10 m channels tests nothing.
#
#    They do survive, today, and here is the measurement to check it against. At metre 32:
#
#      podium_mass   (before the grooves are cut)  51,064,994 voxels   1701.96 m2 of surface
#      podium_stone  (after)                       50,962,476 voxels   1853.05 m2 of surface
#
#    So the grooves take out 102,518 voxels — 3.13 cubic metres of stone — and put back 151.09
#    square metres of surface, which is nine per cent more surface on the part for a third of one
#    per cent less matter. That ratio IS the load. If a change to the sampler makes those two
#    surface figures converge, the grooves have started dissolving, whatever the picture looks
#    like.
#
#    AND EACH GROOVE TAKES OUT ITS OWN SHARE AND NOTHING ELSE, which is a different claim and is
#    the one that had never been checked. `difference { podium_mass podium_rustication
#    podium_paving }` has two cutters in it, and the way that goes wrong is not that a cut is
#    missing — it is that a cut reaches somewhere it was not meant to and nothing in the report
#    says so, because a report cannot tell 3.13 cubic metres taken from the right place from 3.13
#    taken from the wrong one. So the two were sampled separately, in a box cut to this part
#    (`clips/facility/requests/podium-cuts-probe.clip`, `--part probe_pod_pave_only` and
#    `--part probe_pod_rust_only`), and the arithmetic checked:
#
#      metre 32     podium_mass                                  51,064,994 voxels
#                   less the rustication only                    51,037,728     -27,266
#                   less the paving only                         50,989,754     -75,240
#                   less both                                    50,962,488    -102,506 = the sum
#
#      metre 16     podium_mass                                   6,383,040
#                   less the rustication only                     6,379,496      -3,544
#                   less the paving only                          6,372,972     -10,068
#                   less both                                     6,369,428     -13,612 = the sum
#
#    The two cuts are EXACTLY additive at both resolutions — 27,266 + 75,240 = 102,506 and
#    3,544 + 10,068 = 13,612, to the voxel — which is the evidence that they do not overlap and
#    that neither has spilled into ground the other was holding. One component before, one after,
#    in every arm.
#
#    (50,962,488 against the 50,962,476 above is twelve voxels, and it is the two boxes: the
#    figures in the table at the top were taken through the manifest's own bounds and these through
#    the probe's. A different box is a different lattice at the arrises.)
#
# The third load, which comes free with getting the corners right: the whole block is the
# INTERSECTION of two extruded profiles, one drawn in (x, y) and run along z, one drawn in (z, y)
# and run along x. That is what makes every moulding turn all four corners as a true mitre rather
# than as the fat square corner a union of four straight runs gives. It also means fifty million
# voxels are answered by an intersection of two eight-band unions, which is a fair test of box
# culling by itself: at metre 32 it costs 244,498,309 field evaluations of a 582,393,600 cell
# box, so 58% of the box is still being settled whole without ever asking the field.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. M = 0.45, D = 0.90. Every number below is a multiple of M or of M/4 = 0.1125:
#
#   16.65 = 37 M      16.5375 = 36.75 M    16.425 = 36.5 M     the three faces, east and west
#   13.05 = 29 M      12.9375 = 28.75 M    12.825 = 28.5 M     the same three, south
#    8.10 = 18 M       7.9875 = 17.75 M     7.875 = 17.5 M     the same three, north
#
# The main block is x -16.00..16.00 and z -7.50..7.50 by the contract, and those are not module
# numbers, so the terrace ledge round it comes out 0.65 at the sides, 0.60 north and 0.55 south of
# the portico front. The podium is dimensioned by the module and the block sits on it; the other
# way round would have put a number in this file that nothing could be derived from.
#
# WHERE YOUR PART MEETS MINE
#
#   the terrace floor        y = 1.80 everywhere on top. Stand anything on it and sink it 0.05.
#   the south face           z = -13.05 (plinth and corona), z = -12.825 (the rusticated die)
#   the north face           z =  8.10 / 7.875
#   the east and west faces  x = +-16.65 / +-16.425
#   the underside            y = -0.45, buried, for the site to bring its ground up to.
#
#   THE GREAT STEPS: the top tread lands flush at y = 1.80 with its top nosing ON z = -13.05.
#   Ten risers of 0.18 and ten runs of 0.32 is 3.20 m, so the flight runs z = -16.25 (ground) to
#   z = -13.05 (top nosing). That is 0.55 m further south than the contract's steps zone; the
#   alternative is that the last two risers are buried in this slab and the stair arrives at a
#   0.36 m wall. This slab is 0.55 m deeper than the contract's plan because the brief for this
#   part says it must cover z = -13, and it does.
#
#   THAT PARAGRAPH USED TO BE A REQUEST AND IS NOW A FACT. steps.clip was built to the contract
#   and ran -15.70..-12.50, so the corona above swallowed tread ten whole and all but 0.09 m of
#   tread nine and the stair arrived at the terrace over a tread 0.09 deep. site.clip had already
#   been laid out to this file's -13.05 — its gravel, its basins and its return plinths all end
#   there — so two fragments of three agreed and the steps moved. Measured through the pair at
#   metre 32 on the centre line, the top of the matter now runs 1.8125 from z = -13.31 all the
#   way to the terrace with nothing between them but the paving joint at -11.925.
#
#   NOTHING IN THIS FILE CUTS ANYTHING IN THAT ONE, OR THE OTHER WAY ROUND. The only `difference`
#   here takes the two groove unions out of `podium_mass` and out of nothing else, and steps.clip's
#   four take matter out of its own shapes. Neither part exposes a `<part>_void`, so neither is in
#   the manifest's `hollowed` difference at all — which is the one place in this building where a
#   cut belonging to one part can reach another, and is what D608 was.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE SECTION — the same profile on all four sides, 1.80 above ground and 0.45 below
# ================================================================================================
#
#   -0.4500 .. 0.0000   footing, buried, at the plinth face          M
#    0.0000 .. 0.2250   plinth course                                M/2      face 16.65
#    0.2250 .. 0.4500   cyma, plinth face back to the die face       M/2      16.65 -> 16.425
#    0.4500 .. 1.3500   the die — rusticated, two courses of 0.45    2M       face 16.425
#    1.3500 .. 1.4625   ovolo bed mould, die face out again          M/4      16.425 -> 16.5375
#    1.4625 .. 1.5000   fillet                                       M/12     face 16.5375
#    1.5000 .. 1.8000   corona, back out to the plinth face          2M/3     face 16.65
#
# Sum above ground: 0.225 + 0.225 + 0.90 + 0.1125 + 0.0375 + 0.30 = 1.80 exactly.
#
# EVERY CURVED MEMBER IS SQUARE IN SECTION — the cyma is 0.225 out over 0.225 up, the ovolo is
# 0.1125 over 0.1125 — AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THE PROFILE IS SHAPED THIS WAY.
#
# The first two attempts put the die 0.45 in from the plinth: once as a single 0.1125-high cyma
# (four across for one up) and once as two 0.1125-high mouldings (two across for one up). Both
# read on the contact sheet as a little flight of stairs. A voxel grid renders a surface that
# moves further sideways than upward as a run of terraces — at metre 32 a two-to-one curve is
# seven steps two voxels wide each, and the eye reads seven steps. At one to one the terraces are
# one voxel each way and the eye reads a curve. So the projection was cut to 0.225 and the
# mouldings were given their own height back. DO NOT DEEPEN A MOULDING HERE WITHOUT MAKING IT
# TALLER BY THE SAME AMOUNT.
#
# The corona still overhangs, by 0.1125, and its soffit is FLAT — horizontal surfaces voxelize
# exactly, so an undercut costs nothing in terracing and buys the black line under the top of a
# podium, which is the one shadow this part contributes to the scene.
#
# A moulding is only the band between its two corners and the stone behind it is mine, so the die
# box runs from 0.225 up to 1.4625 — through both moulding bands — and each moulding sits outside
# it. FIRST CORNER IN THE STONE, SECOND IN THE AIR: that is the whole of the orientation, and it
# is why the bed mould reads (die face, top) then (fillet face, bottom).
#
# `run=` IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. It defaults to z, and with the default a moulding written along x
# has its section drawn across the whole 33 m of the run and its curve struck in the wrong plane.
# It still fills its own box, so it still looks like a plain fillet, and nothing tells you.

# --- the profile drawn in (x, y), run along the full depth -------------------------------------

let podium_plinth   = box -16.65 -0.45 -13.05   16.65 0.225 8.10 round=0.015
let podium_corona   = box -16.65  1.50 -13.05   16.65 1.80  8.10 round=0.018

let podium_x_die    = box -16.425  0.225  -13.05  16.425  1.4625 8.10 round=0.012
let podium_x_fillet = box -16.5375 1.4625 -13.05  16.5375 1.50   8.10 round=0.010
let podium_x_cyma_e = cyma   16.425 0.225  -13.05   16.65   0.45 8.10 run=z
let podium_x_cyma_w = cyma  -16.425 0.225  -13.05  -16.65   0.45 8.10 run=z
let podium_x_bed_e  = ovolo  16.425 1.4625 -13.05   16.5375 1.35 8.10 run=z
let podium_x_bed_w  = ovolo -16.425 1.4625 -13.05  -16.5375 1.35 8.10 run=z

# THE ORDER OF A UNION IS A SPEED, not a taste. A union evaluates its children in turn and skips
# any whose bounding box is further off than the answer it already holds, so the three bands that
# carry nearly all the surface — corona, die, plinth — are named first and the four small
# mouldings last.
let podium_profile_x = union {
    podium_corona
    podium_x_die
    podium_plinth
    podium_x_fillet
    podium_x_cyma_e podium_x_cyma_w
    podium_x_bed_e  podium_x_bed_w
}

# --- the same profile drawn in (z, y), run along the full width --------------------------------

let podium_z_die    = box -16.65 0.225  -12.825  16.65 1.4625 7.875 round=0.012
let podium_z_fillet = box -16.65 1.4625 -12.9375 16.65 1.50   7.9875 round=0.010
let podium_z_cyma_s = cyma  -16.65 0.225  -12.825  16.65 0.45 -13.05  run=x
let podium_z_cyma_n = cyma  -16.65 0.225    7.875  16.65 0.45   8.10  run=x
let podium_z_bed_s  = ovolo -16.65 1.4625 -12.825  16.65 1.35 -12.9375 run=x
let podium_z_bed_n  = ovolo -16.65 1.4625   7.875  16.65 1.35   7.9875 run=x

let podium_profile_z = union {
    podium_corona
    podium_z_die
    podium_plinth
    podium_z_fillet
    podium_z_cyma_s podium_z_cyma_n
    podium_z_bed_s  podium_z_bed_n
}

# The mitre. Both profiles are solid right across the middle, so away from an edge the
# intersection is simply the other one's profile; at a corner it is the smaller of the two, which
# is exactly what a mitred return is.
let podium_mass = intersection { podium_profile_x podium_profile_z }

# ================================================================================================
# THE RUSTICATION — 0.045 wide, 0.090 deep, two courses of 0.45, laid in running bond
# ================================================================================================
#
# The die is 0.90 tall, so two courses of M, with one channel between them at 0.90. The blocks are
# 1.35 long (3 M), three to one, and the stone at each corner is a different length from the rest
# — 1.575 and 0.90 on the east and west faces, 0.90 and 1.575 on the south and north — because
# THE PATTERN IS SET BY THE MODULE AND STOPPED SHORT OF THE ARRIS, not fitted to the length of the
# face. A joint cut exactly on a corner takes a nick out of the arris from both sides; a joint a
# block short of it leaves a long corner stone, which is what a quoin is anyway. In every case the
# two courses' end stones differ by half a block, which is what running bond means.
#
# One slab repeated along its face and then cut down to a face zone, so a single repeat serves the
# south face and the north face together. These are limited repeats of a child far smaller than
# the period, which is exact and carries a bounding box; nothing here needs `around`, and see
# clips/facility/requests/order.md for what `around` would cost the whole clip.

let podium_joint_x = box -0.0225 -1 -20   0.0225 3 20
let podium_joint_z = box -20 -1 -0.0225   20 3 0.0225

# Lower course: joints every 1.35 out from the centre line, stopping at 14.85. Upper course: the
# same, moved half a block and mirrored, so both halves of the building agree.
let podium_vj_low_x = repeat { podium_joint_x } x=1.35 nx=11
let podium_vj_up_x  = mirror { translate { repeat { podium_joint_x } x=1.35 nx=11 } 0.675 0 0 } axis=x

# In z the die runs -12.825..7.875, which is not a whole number of blocks, so the phase is chosen
# to leave the same end stone at both ends: 0.225 + 1.35k for the lower course and 0.90 + 1.35k
# for the upper. The face zone below cuts off the copies that would land on or near an arris.
let podium_vj_low_z = translate { repeat { podium_joint_z } z=1.35 nz=9 } 0 0 0.225
let podium_vj_up_z  = translate { repeat { podium_joint_z } z=1.35 nz=9 } 0 0 0.90

# The face zones: 0.09 into the stone and 0.05 out into the air, so a channel still has a mouth
# after the manifest's 12 mm of grain has pushed the surface outward.
let podium_face_s_low = box -16.50 0.45   -12.875  16.50 0.9225 -12.735
let podium_face_n_low = box -16.50 0.45     7.785  16.50 0.9225   7.925
let podium_face_s_up  = box -16.50 0.8775 -12.875  16.50 1.35   -12.735
let podium_face_n_up  = box -16.50 0.8775   7.785  16.50 1.35     7.925
let podium_face_e_low = mirror { box 16.335 0.45   -12.375  16.475 0.9225 7.425 } axis=x
let podium_face_e_up  = mirror { box 16.335 0.8775 -12.375  16.475 1.35   7.425 } axis=x

let podium_rust_v_low = intersection { podium_vj_low_x union { podium_face_s_low podium_face_n_low } }
let podium_rust_v_up  = intersection { podium_vj_up_x  union { podium_face_s_up  podium_face_n_up  } }
let podium_rust_v_elo = intersection { podium_vj_low_z podium_face_e_low }
let podium_rust_v_eup = intersection { podium_vj_up_z  podium_face_e_up  }

# The one horizontal channel, at 0.90, right round all four faces and round all four corners.
let podium_rust_h_s = box -16.50 0.8775 -12.875  16.50 0.9225 -12.735
let podium_rust_h_n = box -16.50 0.8775   7.785  16.50 0.9225   7.925
let podium_rust_h_e = mirror { box 16.335 0.8775 -12.90  16.475 0.9225 7.95 } axis=x

let podium_rustication = union {
    podium_rust_h_s podium_rust_h_n podium_rust_h_e
    podium_rust_v_low podium_rust_v_up
    podium_rust_v_elo podium_rust_v_eup
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE TERRACE PAVING — 1.35 m slabs, joints 0.0375 wide and 0.045 deep
# ================================================================================================
#
# The whole top is paved, not only the part outside the walls, because which part is outside the
# walls is not knowable from this file and a joint under a wall costs nothing and is never seen.
#
# The grid is offset half a slab from the centre line in x, so that the six portico columns —
# whose axes stand at 1.35, 4.05 and 6.75, all odd multiples of the slab — each stand in the
# middle of a stone rather than astride a joint. In z the phase is picked to match, and both
# leave a plain margin course 1.125 (2.5 M) wide the whole way round, over the cornice, which is
# what the edge of a terrace wants.
#
# 1.755 IS THE FLOOR OF THE JOINT AND IT IS THE DEEPEST ANYTHING CUTS INTO THIS SLAB FROM ABOVE.
# The podium is hollow after all — crypt.clip carves a room out of it in the manifest's `hollowed`
# difference, and that room's soffit is at 1.6875 (1.80 less M/4, chosen to land on a voxel
# boundary at metre 16 and 32 alike). So between the floor of a paving joint and the ceiling of the
# crypt underneath it there is 0.0675 m of granite, two voxels at metre 32. A joint 0.1125 deep
# instead of 0.045 would open the terrace into the crypt along every line of the paving grid, and
# it would do it silently: the volume would move by three cubic metres out of fifteen hundred, the
# component count would not change at all, and the only sign of it would be daylight in a room
# whose whole brief is that light reaches it through two stair wells and nothing else.
let podium_pave_x = box -0.01875 1.755 -20   0.01875 1.82 20
let podium_pave_z = box -20 1.755 -0.01875   20 1.82 0.01875

let podium_paving = union {
    mirror { translate { repeat { podium_pave_x } x=1.35 nx=11 } 0.675 0 0 } axis=x
    translate { repeat { podium_pave_z } z=1.35 nz=9 } 0 0 0.225
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let podium_stone = difference { podium_mass podium_rustication podium_paving }

let part_podium = podium_stone

# Nothing is hollowed out of the podium — it is solid stone all the way through, which is the
# whole of its first job. There is no `void_podium` and the manifest does not ask for one.

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule is keyed on a zone INTERSECTED WITH MY OWN STONE, so no rule can reach a voxel that
# belongs to somebody else even where their geometry passes through the same coordinates. And
# every rule is below=0.02, never below=0, because the manifest displaces the whole building by
# 12 mm at the end and a test at zero leaves a rind of the base coat over every outward face.
#
# A dark hard base, a pale cornice, a white floor: granite up to the top of the die, limestone for
# the cornice, marble where it is walked, with the cornice's own top left as a limestone margin
# course round the marble. Three materials on one piece of stone, meeting along lines that are
# horizontal to within a voxel — a fair test of whether a paint rule's boundary lands where its
# shape says it does.
#
# Everything from the footing to the top of the die is ONE material and ONE zone on purpose:
# 1293.4 of the 1555.3 cubic metres in this part, against 261.7 for the cornice and 73.1 for the
# marble floor inside it. That 1293 is the uniform-brick case the first note at the top of this
# file is about. Splitting it for the sake of variety would cost the scene the only large
# single-material volume it has.

let podium_zone_base    = intersection { podium_stone  box -17 -0.50 -13.10  17 1.3500 8.15 }
let podium_zone_cornice = intersection { podium_stone  box -17  1.3500 -13.10  17 1.9000 8.15 }
let podium_zone_terrace = intersection { podium_stone  box -15.525 1.6875 -11.925  15.525 1.9000 6.975 }

paint granite   where=podium_zone_base    below=0.02
paint limestone where=podium_zone_cornice below=0.02
paint marble    where=podium_zone_terrace below=0.02
